# Makes GitHub render a "Cite this repository" button, and gives anybody
# quoting these figures a fixed thing to point at.
#
# The distinction this file has to carry: almost nothing here was measured by
# this project. Advisory counts are OSV's, scorecards are Google Open Source
# Insights', end-of-life dates are endoflife.date's, incidents are the
# providers' own announcements, publish dates are the registries'. What is
# original is the collection, the dating, and the keeping — a record of when
# each of those said what, held after they stopped holding it themselves.
#
# So a paper citing a scorecard should cite Open Source Insights. A paper
# citing "what this said on 12 March" cites this.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Sighttrue: a dated public record of open-source dependency and provider readings"
message: "If you use this data, please cite it and the underlying sources named below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: "Sighttrue"
    website: "https://sighttrue.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sighttrue/sighttrue"
url: "https://sighttrue.com"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A four-hourly record of eleven readings across roughly four hundred
  hand-picked open-source repositories and the services they depend on:
  releases, fork activity, dependency manifests, OpenSSF scorecards, OSV
  advisory counts, registry publish dates, install-time scripts, publisher
  withdrawals, commit concentration, language-model prices, end-of-life dates,
  and the incident histories of twenty providers kept after their own status
  pages drop them. Every reading is committed to a public git repository as it
  is taken, so any figure can be checked against the commit that recorded it.
keywords:
  - open source
  - software supply chain
  - dependencies
  - package registries
  - end-of-life
  - incident history
  - reproducible data
